        <mxCell id="steps_box" parent="1" style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=6;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=12;spacing=8;verticalAlign=middle;align=center;fillColor=#F7F9FC;strokeColor=#6C8EBF;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;spacing=12;fontColor=#333333;" value="&lt;b&gt;ONBOARDING&lt;/b&gt; &lt;font style=&#39;font-size:10px&#39; color=&#39;#777777&#39;&gt;(operator, one-time setup)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;① Register a new ERP connection — erp_type, base_url, credentials. Once per ERP instance.&lt;br&gt;② Set the subsidiary's current route to that connection. One active route per subsidiary at a time — changeable later.&lt;br&gt;③ Bind each reseller who trades with that subsidiary to the connection, recording their erp_customer_id inside it. A reseller trading with two subsidiaries on two different ERPs needs two bindings.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEN, AUTOMATICALLY AT RUNTIME&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;④ Issuing a quote for a subsidiary stamps its &lt;i&gt;current&lt;/i&gt; route onto the quote, permanently — changing the route later never moves quotes already issued.&lt;br&gt;⑤ Placing an order inherits the quote's stamp. Before accepting it, the system resolves the reseller's erp_customer_id for that exact connection and refuses the order if no verified binding exists — this lookup is the &lt;b&gt;entity resolver&lt;/b&gt;: the same reseller can be a completely different customer number in each ERP they're onboarded into." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="770" width="1180" height="200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
